-## GM-5 joint module                                                           
+## GM-5 Joint Module                                                           
                                                                                
 The four big joints in each arm use the same printed module. A Feetech STS3250 
drives a 12-tooth sun, three 18-tooth planets and a 48-tooth ring cut into the  
housing (module 1.0, 5:1). The carrier runs in a 6807 bearing and carries the   
same 6 × M3 output flange as the arm links. A hall sensor in the housing and a  
magnet in the carrier home each joint at power-up, because the servo's own      
encoder repeats every 72° of output. Print the gears in PETG-CF or nylon if you 
can. `cad/gearbox.py` has the geometry and `cad/stl/gm_*.stl` the files.        
                                                                                
-## Head limits                                                                 
+## Head Limits                                                                 
                                                                                
 The head pans ±0.6 rad and tilts −0.35 to +0.55 rad. Below 0.51 m of lift, the 
software must not combine full pan with looking up, because the back of the head
would reach the mast. The CAD check tests full pan at level gaze, full tilt up  
and down, and pan with tilt down.                                               
                                                                                
 ## Actuators                                                                   
                                                                                
 | Joint | Actuator | Rated / peak | Worst gravity load, 0.5 kg in the hand |   
 | --- | --- | --- | --- |                                                      
 | Shoulder pitch (J1) | GM-5: STS3250 + printed 5:1 planetary | 6.7 / 20.8 N·m 
| about 0.6 m/s |                                                               
                                                                                
 The torques come from `sim/check.py`. It runs MuJoCo inverse dynamics on the   
CAD masses at the worst pose for each joint.                                    
                                                                                
